🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“Your production server is running out of disk space. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately delete files.

First, I want to understand what is consuming the disk space.

I would start with:

df -h

This tells me which filesystem is running out of space.

Then I would identify the directories consuming the most space:

du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h

If /var is consuming significant space, I would investigate further:

du -sh /var/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h

Then I would check common causes such as:

• Application logs
• Large temporary files
• Old backup files
• Core dumps
• Package/cache files
• Large application data

I would also check for deleted files that are still being held open by a running process:

lsof +L1

This is an important check because df can show high disk usage even when the corresponding file is no longer visible in the filesystem.

I would also check inode usage:

df -i

Sometimes the disk has free space, but the server has run out of inodes because of a huge number of small files.

Once I identify the root cause, I would follow the approved cleanup or recovery procedure.

For example, if old logs are consuming space, I would not randomly delete active log files. I would use the organization's log rotation, retention, or cleanup process.

After cleanup, I would verify:

df -h
df -i

Then I would check whether the application and server are functioning normally.

Finally, I would document:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

For prevention, I might consider proper log rotation, retention policies, monitoring alerts, and capacity planning.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

When disk usage reaches 100%, don't just start deleting files.

First understand:

Which filesystem → What is consuming space → Why is it growing → What is safe to clean → How do we prevent it?

That's the difference between running commands and troubleshooting a production system. 
